
{"id":"f90356bd-ca0a-4217-b8f1-4404c395a303","object":"chat.completion","created":1785002586,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\\n        children.setdefault(nid, [])\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        parent = n.get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    retained = set()\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if not n[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            retained.add(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    removable = [(n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]) for n in nodes if n[\\\"removable\\\"]]\\n    removable.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for depth, nid in removable:\\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\\n        has_retained_child = any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            retained.add(nid)\\n            continue\\n        sym = node[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        if sym in required_symbols:\\n            count = sum(1 for rid in retained if node_by_id[rid][\\\"symbol\\\"] == sym)\\n            if count == 0:\\n                retained.add(nid)\\n                continue\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_node_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for SE-PE-01. The task is to perform grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree, preserving required symbols and interestingness. The output schema requires two arrays: removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of IDs of nodes that remain).\n\nGiven the payload: nodes list with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility; required_symbols list. The root has parent null. The goal is to remove nodes that are removable (removable=True) while ensuring required symbols are represented in the remaining tree. The root and nodes with non-removable are kept. Also, we should not remove a node if it has retained children (i.e., if we remove a node, its children would be orphaned? The description says: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" This is key: we cannot remove a node that has children that are retained (i.e., the node's children that are not also removed). But we can remove a node if its entire subtree is removed? However the tree structure: nodes have parent references. If we remove a node, its children become orphans; presumably they are also removed? The output only lists IDs of removed nodes and retained nodes. The instruction \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" suggests that if a node has any child that is retained, we cannot remove it. So we must remove nodes from leaves upward, ensuring each removal doesn't leave orphaned retained children.\n\nAlso, tentative removal requires all required symbols remain represented. So after removal, the set of symbols in the retained tree must include the required_symbols.\n\nThe ordering: deeper nodes before shallower nodes; use node ID as tie break at equal depth. So we should process nodes in descending depth, then ascending ID (or any stable order). The removal order is the order in which we actually remove nodes. We need to output removed_node_ids in that order.\n\nWe must return retained_node_ids sorted (presumably ascending) and removed_node_ids in removal order.\n\nThe algorithm: \n1. Build a tree structure: map node id to node dict; also map parent id to children list. \n2. Identify initial retained set: all nodes that are not removable (removable=False) are automatically retained. Also, note: root is removable=False.\n3. For each required_symbol, ensure at least one node with that symbol is retained.\n   But we can also keep removable nodes if needed for required symbols.\n4. The reduction: we want to remove as many removable nodes as possible while preserving required symbols and not removing nodes with retained children.\n   This is essentially: for each removable node (candidate for removal), we tentatively check if removing it (and possibly its descendants?) would still leave required symbols and that it has no retained children. But careful: if we remove a node, its children are also removed (since they become orphaned). However the rule says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So we can only remove a node if all its children are also being removed (i.e., they are not retained). So effectively, we can remove a node only if its subtree (including itself) is entirely removed. So reduction is a process of pruning subtrees: we can remove a node if its entire subtree (including itself) is removable and removing it doesn't remove required symbols. But the node itself must be removable.\n\nBut the rule \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has a child that is not being removed, we cannot remove that node. So we need to decide which removable nodes to keep and which to remove. The typical approach: from leaves upward (deeper first), decide for each removable node whether to remove it or keep it. But we must ensure that if we keep a node, its parent cannot be removed if that parent has other retained children? Actually, rule: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if we keep a child (i.e., child is retained), then parent cannot be removed. But if we decide to remove the child, then parent might become removable if it has no other retained children. So we need to propagate decisions.\n\nAlternatively, we can treat each removable node as either retained or removed. The constraints:\n- The root is retained (not removable).\n- A node with removable=True can be removed only if all nodes in its subtree that are not removable are retained? Actually, if we remove a node, all its descendants are also removed (they become orphaned). But we cannot remove a node if any descendant is retained. Because those descendants would be orphaned and should be retained according to our decision? The rule says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" It doesn't explicitly say about deeper descendants, but if a child is retained, then the node has a retained child, so cannot be removed. So to remove a node, its entire subtree must be removed as well. That implies the decision to remove a subtree is all-or-nothing for that subtree.\n- Additionally, the set of symbols present in retained nodes must include all required_symbols.\n\nThus problem reduces: we have a tree with root. Each node has a boolean 'removable' (True/False). For each removable node, we can either keep it (retained) or remove it (and its entire subtree). But note: if we keep a removable node, its ancestors up to the root cannot be removed (because they have a retained child? Actually, if a node is kept, its parent has a retained child, so parent cannot be removed. This propagates upward). So the set of retained nodes must be a connected subtree containing root (by definition, since root is retained and each node's parent is either retained or the node is root). Actually, if we keep a node, its parent must also be kept? No, parent could be removed if we remove the node? But if we remove parent, then the node would be removed too. So the set of retained nodes must be a rooted subtree: if a node is retained, its parent must be retained (unless it's root) because otherwise parent would have been removed and node would be orphaned. So the retained nodes form a connected subtree from root.\n\nSo decision: we need to select a subtree (connected from root) that includes all required symbols and is maximal in size? Actually, we want to remove as many nodes as possible, so we want the smallest possible subtree that still contains required symbols and respects that non-removable nodes must be retained. So we want to remove all removable nodes that are not necessary to cover required symbols.\n\nStrategy: start with all nodes that are forced to be retained: root, all non-removable nodes. Also, we must retain at least one node for each required symbol. For each required symbol, we need to ensure that symbol is present in the retained set. If already present in forced retained, fine. Otherwise, we need to select some removable node(s) with that symbol to retain. There may be multiple nodes with that symbol. We want to choose a set that minimizes total retained nodes (to maximize removal). This is like a set cover on tree with depth constraints? But the tree structure imposes that retaining a node forces retaining its entire path to root. So essentially, to cover a required symbol, we can pick a node with that symbol; retaining it adds that node and all its ancestors (which may already be retained). The cost in terms of extra nodes retained is the node plus any ancestors not already retained.\n\nWe can approach greedily: process nodes from deepest to shallowest (as described). For each removable node in that order, tentatively try to remove it (and its entire subtree). But we can only remove a node if after removal, all required symbols are still represented by the remaining tree. And also, removal must be valid: node itself removable, and it has no retained children (since we process from deep to shallow, children decisions are already made). So we can implement a recursive or iterative removal: initially mark all non-removable as retained. Then for each removable node in decreasing depth (and ID tiebreak), check if removing it (i.e., not retaining it and all its descendants that are removable? Actually, if we remove a node, we must remove its entire subtree. But we only consider removal if the node is not already determined to be retained (e.g., because of required symbol). Since we process from deepest to shallowest, we will have already decided on its children. If any child is retained, then this node cannot be removed (because it has retained children). So we can only consider removal if none of its children are retained (i.e., all children are either non-removable (and thus retained) or if they are removable and we have decided to remove them? But children could be retained because of required symbols. So we need to first decide symbol coverage.\n\nSimpler approach: we can compute the minimal set of nodes that must be retained to cover required symbols, while respecting connectivity. Then all other removable nodes can be removed. This is essentially: retain all non-removable nodes. Then for each required symbol not yet covered, select one node with that symbol that is deepest (to minimize additional ancestors?). Actually, we want to minimize total retained nodes, so we want to select nodes that share common ancestors. Greedy from the deepest nodes might work: iterate over required symbols, for each uncov symbol, find the deepest node with that symbol that is not already scheduled for removal (i.e., consider all nodes with that symbol, and choose one that is deepest in the tree and also whose path to root has the fewest new nodes?). Alternatively, we can use a dynamic programming or topological sort.\n\nGiven the small size of tree (7 nodes in examples), maybe a brute force search over all subsets of removable nodes? That could be exponential but fine for small trees. But we don't know actual input size; however it's a public fixture with small trees, but production might be larger. The task says \"grammar-guided node reduction on a miniature grammar\", so likely small trees (<50 nodes). We can implement a simple algorithm: sort removable nodes by depth descending, then ID ascending, and for each, try to mark it as removed if possible, and if not, keep it. This is a heuristic but might be correct for this problem? Let's test with the given fixtures. In first case: nodes: root (depth0, non-removable), n1 (depth1, symbol s1, non-removable), n2 (depth1, s2, removable), n3 (depth1, s0, non-removable), n4 (depth2, s1, removable), n5 (depth2, s2, non-removable), n6 (depth2, s0, removable). required_symbols: root, s1.\n\nNon-removable: root, n1, n3, n5. So retained initially: root, n1, n3, n5. Required symbols: root is present, s1 is present in n1 (non-removable). So all required symbols are already covered. Now we can try to remove removable nodes in descending depth: depth2: n4 (removable, symbol s1), n6 (removable, s0). Check removal of n4: does it have retained children? It has no children (depth2). After removal, required symbols still covered (s1 still in n1). So we can remove n4. Similarly n6: removable, no children, required s1 still there, remove n6. Then depth1: n2 (removable, s2). Does it have retained children? Its child is n4, which we just removed (so not retained). So no retained children. Required symbols still covered? s1 still present. So remove n2. So removal order: n4, n6, n2. Retained nodes: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted IDs: n1, n3, n5, root? Actually root is also retained. So \"retained_node_ids\" should be a list of strings, presumably sorted alphabetically? The fixture doesn't specify sort order, but output schema says \"retained_node_ids\" array of strings, no explicit order requirement? The candidate artifact says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". So we should sort them ascending lexicographically. So root, n1, n3, n5 in order. That matches plausible expected output.\n\nSecond case: similar, but n1 has utility 0.500... etc, but same structure. Non-removable same nodes. So same output.\n\nThird case: same structure. So all three cases would produce removed_node_ids: [\"n4\",\"n6\",\"n2\"] and retained: [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"] sorted.\n\nIs that correct? Let's consider if there are multiple nodes with required symbol, and we might need to keep a removable node to satisfy required symbol if the non-removable nodes don't cover it. For example, if the required symbols included \"s2\", then non-removable nodes: n5 is s2, so covered. But if required included \"s0\", then n3 is s0 (non-removable) and n6 is also s0 (removable). Already covered. So examples cover that.\n\nBut what if a required symbol is only present in removable nodes? E.g., suppose required \"s2\" but n5 was removable? In fixture, n5 is non-removable. But the algorithm must handle that. The approach of processing deeper first and tentatively removing if required symbols still present seems to work: we keep the deepest node that provides the required symbol, because we process deeper first; if we remove it, we would lose that symbol; but we cannot remove it because required symbols check fails. So it would be kept. That's correct.\n\nBut we must also ensure that we don't remove a node that has retained children. That condition is automatically satisfied if we process from leaves upward: when we consider a node, its children have already been decided (retained or removed). So we check if any child is retained; if yes, we cannot remove this node. So we must keep it.\n\nThus the algorithm:\n- Build tree: map id -> node dict; also maintain list of children per node.\n- Initialize retained set with all nodes where removable=False.\n- Also, initialize a set of required symbols to cover from required_symbols list.\n- Compute initial coverage: for each node in retained set, add its symbol to covered_symbols.\n- Then, create list of removable nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending (stable).\n- Process each node in that order:\n   - Skip if node already in retained (should not happen because we only process removable).\n   - Check if node is removable (it is).\n   - Check if any of its children are retained (if children list and any child id in retained set). If yes, skip (cannot remove).\n   - Check if removing this node (and its entire subtree) would cause any required symbol to become uncovered. Since we remove only this node and its descendants (but descendants that are retained will have already been considered and either kept or removed; but if we remove this node, all descendants that are retained will become orphaned? However our check of children retained should ensure no children retained. But deeper descendants could be retained even if children are not? Actually, if no children are retained, then there are no retained descendants either, because to have a retained descendant, must have a child retained. So condition of no retained children suffices. So removal of node removes only itself and any removable descendants that were already removed? But those descendants are not retained. So effectively, only the node itself is removed from retained set (if not already removed). So we need to check if removing this node would leave required symbols uncovered. Compute: after removal, the retained set would be current retained set minus this node (if currently considered not retained). But we haven't added it yet, so we only need to check if this node's symbol is required and currently only this node covers it. Since we process from deep to shallow, we may have already kept some other node providing that symbol. So we need to check: if this node's symbol is in required_symbols and there is no other retained node (including non-removable and removable retained from earlier) that has that symbol, then we cannot remove it. So we must keep it (add to retained). Otherwise, we can remove it (i.e., not add to retained).\n   - If can remove: we add node id to removed_node_ids list (in order of removal). Do not add to retained set.\n   - If cannot remove: add node id to retained set (since we need to keep it).\n- After processing all removable nodes, the retained set now contains all nodes that are kept.\n- Output removed_node_ids in order of removal, and retained_node_ids sorted (we can sort lexically, e.g., naturally sorted as strings; typical IDs like \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"n6\",\"root\" sort correctly).\n- Return JSON.\n\nThis algorithm should work for the given fixtures. However we need to verify edge cases: root has no parent, non-removable. If required_symbols includes a symbol only present in a removable node that is deep, our algorithm will keep it because when we encounter that node, we check coverage: if no other retained node has that symbol, we cannot remove it, so we keep it. Good.\n\nBut what if a node has retained children? Then we cannot remove it, but we also don't need to check coverage because it's forced kept. So we keep it.\n\nPotential bug: we must ensure that when we keep a node due to required symbol, its children (which are deeper) have already been processed and may have been removed. That's fine because the node is retained, so its children are still attached. But if a child was removed, that child's subtree is gone. That's valid: we keep the parent but some children are removed. That's allowed because rule only says never remove a node with retained children, but it's fine to keep a node while removing some of its children. So we can have a parent kept while some children removed. This is consistent with Perses-like reduction where you can delete subtrees.\n\nThus algorithm is correct.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Parse case: case is the payload dict; it has keys \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\" and \"_case_nonce\".\n- Build node_by_id dict.\n- Build children lists: for each node, add child id to parent's list.\n- Initialize retained set: all ids where node[\"removable\"] == False.\n- covered_symbols set: union of node[\"symbol\"] for all ids in retained.\n- removable_nodes = [n for n in node_by_id if node_by_id[n][\"removable\"]]\n- Sort removable_nodes by (-depth, id) using node[\"depth\"].\n- removed_order = []\n- For each id in sorted_removable:\n   - node = node_by_id[id]\n   - if any child in node_children[id] and child in retained: continue (cannot remove, so keep it)\n   - Check if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n        # Count how many retained nodes have this symbol currently. Since we haven't added this node to retained, we consider only current retained.\n        count = sum(1 for r in retained if node_by_id[r][\"symbol\"] == node[\"symbol\"])\n        # If count == 0, then this node is needed to cover the required symbol. So we must keep it.\n        if count == 0:\n            retained.add(id)\n            continue\n   - # Otherwise, we can remove\n   - removed_order.append(id)\n- At the end, retained set includes all initially retained plus any removable nodes we kept.\n- retained_node_ids = sorted(retained) (lexicographic string sort).\n- Return dict with \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids.\n\nNeed to ensure we handle case when there are no removable nodes or all removable are removed.\n\nAlso, should we include nodes that are not removable but were never in retained? They are in retained initially, so fine.\n\nEdge case: root is non-removable, but we might accidentally keep it? It's in retained initially.\n\nNow package as implementation.\n\nWe must return exactly a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing Python source as a string.\n\nI'll write the function solve(case) accordingly.\n\nMake sure to import nothing beyond standard library.\n\nAlso, ensure no mutation of case.\n\nWrite as:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # Build maps\n    node_by_id = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\n        children.setdefault(nid, [])\n    for n in nodes:\n        parent = n.get(\"parent\")\n        if parent is not None:\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(n[\"id\"])\n    # Retained initially: non-removable\n    retained = set()\n    for n in nodes:\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            retained.add(n[\"id\"])\n    # Sorted removable list by depth desc, then id asc\n    removable = [(n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]) for n in nodes if n[\"removable\"]]\n    removable.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\n    removed_order = []\n    for depth, nid in removable:\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\n        # Check children retained\n        has_retained_child = any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, []))\n        if has_retained_child:\n            retained.add(nid)\n            continue\n        # Check if symbol is required and if removal would lose it\n        sym = node[\"symbol\"]\n        if sym in required_symbols:\n            # count how many retained nodes (excluding this one) have this symbol\n            count = sum(1 for rid in retained if node_by_id[rid][\"symbol\"] == sym)\n            if count == 0:\n                retained.add(nid)\n                continue\n        # Remove\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    # Remove any nodes that are in removed_order from retained (they might have been added inadvertently? Actually we only add to retained when cannot remove, so fine)\n    # No need to remove, but ensure consistency.\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids\n    }\n\nBut we need to be careful: the removable nodes that we keep (due to required symbol or retained children) are added to retained set. Those that are removed are not added. After loop, retained contains all nodes to keep. The removed_order list is the removal order.\n\nNow test on given fixture.\n\nWe'll validate manually.\n\nFirst case: nodes: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6. non-removable: root, n1, n3, n5. retained initially includes those. removable: n2, n4, n6. sorted depth: depth2: n4, n6 (depth2). For n4: children? none. has_retained_child? no. sym=s1. required symbols includes s1. count of s1 in retained: only n1 (non-removable) is retained and has s1 => count=1 >0, so can remove. removed_order = [\"n4\"]. Then n6: sym=s0, not required => can remove. removed_order = [\"n4\",\"n6\"]. Then depth1: n2. children: n4 is removed (not in retained). has_retained_child? no. sym=s2, not required => remove. removed_order = [\"n4\",\"n6\",\"n2\"]. retained set still has root,n1,n3,n5. sorted retained = [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. Nice.\n\nSecond/third cases same.\n\nNow consider scenario where required symbol only present on removable node. For example, set required_symbols = [\"s2\"] and make n5 removable (hypothetically). Then non-removable: root, n1, n3. retained initially: root, n1, n3. Then process removable: first n4 (s1, not required) - remove. n6 (s0) - remove. n2 (s2) - check: children? none (n4 removed). has_retained_child false. sym s2 in required. Count of s2 in retained: 0 (since n5 also removable, but hasn't been processed yet; note n5 is depth2 and also removable, should be processed before n2? Actually depth2 nodes come first: n4, n5, n6. n5 has sym s2. So when processing n5: children? none. has_retained false. sym s2 required. count=0 => keep n5. retained adds n5. Then later n2: sym s2, count now =1 (n5). So can remove n2. So result: removed n4,n6,n2; retained root,n1,n3,n5. That's correct: we kept the deeper n5 to cover s2, removed n2. That minimizes retained nodes.\n\nThus algorithm works.\n\nNow, regarding the candidate_artifact text: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes. Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" We follow that.\n\nNow encode as JSON string. We need to produce a single JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string. The Python source must be proper and indentation retained. We'll write the function definition with appropriate indentation. Then stringify, escaping quotes and newlines.\n\nWe'll use triple-quoted string? The output must be a JSON string, so we need to escape. We'll write the code normally then use json.dumps to produce the string. But since we are generating the response manually, we can just write the JSON object with escaped newlines and quotes.\n\nI'll produce the final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":5935,"total_tokens":8104,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5533},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}